98-- **Timestamps:** 2:52–3:18 (rule stated 2:43–2:52)
99-- **Mechanics:** The winner-take-all rule is spoken over the margin field — "If they win the state by 1%, they win 100% of the electoral votes" (transcript 2:51) — then priced in votes: horizontal red bars rank Trump's votes by state (Texas, Florida, California…), and California's bar carries its number on its face: 4,484,000 — votes that "didn't matter" (transcript 3:07). Binding: the rule is a verbal claim bound to transcript 2:43–2:52; its price is the visual claim bound to this frame. No flip animation exists to cite — the erasure is shown as a static ranked bar, which is itself the finding (§5, B-form note in T13's lesson L4).
100-- **Why it works:** An abstract allocation rule becomes a counted pile of discarded votes in the state where the loser won the most of them.
101-- **Evidence:** ev/t185-california-votes.jpg (ranked bars, California labelled 4,484,000, 185 s)
